correction.en:
Interpretation
- fbData.verdict: a short, machine-checked code describing the outcome (e.g., "approve", "reject", "needs_changes").
- fbData.msg: human-readable explanatory message to show to users or store in logs.

Best practices + concrete example (JavaScript)
- Validate that verdict is one of allowed values.
- Sanitize fbData.msg before displaying or storing.
- Map verdict to application action (status, notifications).
- Log or persist both fields for audit.

Working example
```js
function escapeHtml(s) {
  return String(s)
    .replace(/&/g, "&amp;")
    .replace(/</g, "&lt;")
    .replace(/>/g, "&gt;")
    .replace(/"/g, "&quot;")
    .replace(/'/g, "&#39;");
}

const ALLOWED = new Set(['approve','reject','needs_changes']);

function handleFbData(fbData) {
  if (!fbData || typeof fbData.verdict !== 'string') throw new Error('invalid fbData');
  const verdict = fbData.verdict.toLowerCase();
  if (!ALLOWED.has(verdict)) throw new Error('unsupported verdict');

  const safeMsg = escapeHtml(fbData.msg || '');
  let status, notify;
  switch (verdict) {
    case 'approve':
      status = 'published'; notify = true; break;
    case 'reject':
      status = 'rejected'; notify = true; break;
    case 'needs_changes':
      status = 'changes_requested'; notify = true; break;
  }
  // persist status and safeMsg, send notifications as needed
  return { status, message: safeMsg, notify };
}

// Example:
handleFbData({ verdict: 'approve', msg: 'Looks good <script>' });
```

Risks to consider: injection via msg, missing/unknown verdicts, forged feedback (authenticate source).

correction.fr:
Attendu d’une bonne réponse
- Interpréter fbData.verdict comme un code d’état (enum) et fbData.msg comme texte d’accompagnement ; afficher sous la forme "VERDICT: message" ou mapper le verdict à un libellé lisible.
- Valider et normaliser : verdict ∈ {approved, rejected, pending} (ou autre ensemble défini) ; message non vide, longueur limitée, échappement HTML.
- Gérer les cas invalides : valeurs manquantes → fallback clair ("unknown", "Aucun message").
- Séparer logique (validation/mapping) et présentation (UI/formatage).
- Prévoir internationalisation et accessibilité (libellés traduits, rôles ARIA).

Risques concrets
- Injection XSS si fbData.msg affiché sans échappement.
- Valeurs inattendues provoquant plantages ou comportements incohérents.
- Messages trop longs rompant le layout.
- Mauvaise interprétation du verdict entraînant actions erronées.

Exemple JavaScript (Node/Frontend) — fonction sûre et testable
const VALID = ['approved','rejected','pending'];
function formatFeedback(fbData){
  const verdict = (fbData && VALID.includes(fbData.verdict)) ? fbData.verdict : 'unknown';
  let msg = (fbData && typeof fbData.msg === 'string') ? fbData.msg.trim() : '';
  if(!msg) msg = 'No message provided';
  // simple truncation + HTML-escape
  const escaped = msg.replace(/[&<>"']/g, c=>({ '&':'&amp;','<':'&lt;','>':'&gt;','"':'&quot;',"'":'&#39;' }[c]));
  return `${verdict.toUpperCase()}: ${escaped.slice(0,500)}`;
}

Tests à inclure
- verdict valide/invalide, msg vide, msg contenant HTML, msg très long.

